---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import CaseStudyCard from '@/components/CaseStudyCard.astro';
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '@/components/PageHero.astro';
import CtaPanel from '@/components/CtaPanel.astro';
import { href } from '@/lib/url';
import {
  graph,
  organizationSchema,
  webSiteSchema,
  webPageSchema,
  breadcrumbSchema,
  itemListSchema,
} from '@/lib/schema';

const title = 'Clinic Website Examples: 4 Sites You Can Open | CareInflow';
const description =
  'Websites built by CareInflow: a live bilingual dental clinic in Mehsana, plus dermatology, physiotherapy and services samples you can open. Three are samples.';

const caseStudies = (await getCollection('caseStudies')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);

const schema = graph(
  organizationSchema(),
  webSiteSchema(),
  webPageSchema({ path: '/work', title, description, type: 'CollectionPage' }),
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Portfolio', path: '/work' },
  ]),
  itemListSchema({
    path: '/work',
    name: 'CareInflow case studies',
    items: caseStudies.map((cs) => ({ name: cs.data.title, path: `/work/${cs.id}` })),
  }),
);
---

<BaseLayout {title} {description} path="/work" {schema}>
  <PageHero
    label="Our portfolio"
    title="Clinic websites we have built. Do not take our word for it."
    lede="Every site on this page is live right now. Open one on your phone, time how long it takes to appear, tap the WhatsApp button and watch where the message goes. One was built for a real practice. The other three are samples, built to show a clinic exactly what it receives, and each says so at the top of its own page. Our second client, Akshar Wellness in Mehsana, is social media work rather than a build, so it does not appear here."
    stats={[
      { value: 'Live', caption: 'open every one on your own phone' },
      { value: 'Healthcare', caption: 'the only work this studio takes' },
      { value: 'Fixed', caption: 'price agreed before anything starts' },
    ]}
  />

  {/*
    Identical cards, two to a row.

    Two earlier arrangements were wrong in the same way. Giving the flagship
    full width and the others `max-w-[760px]` left ~500px of dead ground down
    the page; pairing two under a full-width lead fixed the ragged edge but
    still sized the cards by how important we consider each project, which is
    a judgement a visitor has not asked for. Equal cards let the work argue
    for itself, and the eye can compare them at one size.

    THE COLUMN COUNT HAS TO DIVIDE THE PROJECT COUNT, which is the grid rule
    here. At three projects that meant three columns; the fourth arrived and
    three columns would have stranded it alone on a second row, reading as a
    page that ran out. Four into two divides evenly, so `xl:grid-cols-3` came
    off and the grid stops at two. It is also the roomier answer — a card at
    xl went from a 352px column to ~660px — and it keeps a fifth project from
    breaking the page again, since 2x2 becomes 3x2 without a decision.

    Below sm they stack. Three columns at tablet width left ~210px per card,
    which is narrower than this content survives, and two columns there is
    already the shape the page settles into.

    Card heights match because the grid stretches every item in a row and each
    card is a flex column with `mt-auto` on the measurements, so the numbers
    and the link sit on a shared baseline however long a summary runs.

    THE CARD IS COMPOSED PER SCREEN, not scaled. Stacked on a phone, the
    desktop composition measured 807px — 116% of a 696px viewport — so one card
    filled the screen and a visitor never saw that a second existed. On a phone
    the card is a summary: shorter band, tighter padding, the summary clamped,
    and two measurements instead of four. The rest is one tap away in the case
    study, which is where it was always going to be read properly.
  */}
  <section class="band-powder" aria-label="Case studies">
    <div class="section shell grid items-stretch gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {caseStudies.map((cs) => <CaseStudyCard study={cs} />)}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="max-w-[680px] rounded-3xl border border-line bg-surface p-8">
      <h2 class="label-mono mb-4 text-faint">What would yours involve?</h2>
      <p class="text-[16px] leading-[1.65] text-body">
        Every build runs the same way: one fixed price agreed in writing before
        anything starts, the person building your site on the other end of
        WhatsApp, and a written record when it launches. Four weeks, and the
        study in the first week is free whether or not you go ahead.
        The <a href={href('/about')} class="prose-link">about page</a>
        explains who is behind the work, and the
        <a href={href('/process')} class="prose-link">method page</a> shows how a
        four-week build runs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaPanel prefill="work" nextStep={{ label: 'See what it costs', href: '/pricing' }} />
</BaseLayout>
